WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.600
Nobody likes a bad movie, but nobody hates a&nbsp;
flop any more than the stars themselves. Whip out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.554
some rotten tomatoes, because these major movie&nbsp;
stars got defensive over their box office bombs.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.960
It's no surprise that Hollywood's always&nbsp;
been enamored with the tale of Robin Hood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.700
A do-gooder who battles a tyrannical villain to&nbsp;
help the less fortunate, Robin Hood is a medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:26.400
superhero — but reimagining folk tales as gritty&nbsp;
action movies seems like a bit of a tired idea.

00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:29.023
"Really inspiring. Thanks."

00:00:29.023 --> 00:00:34.440
So in 2018, when Robin Hood hit theaters&nbsp;
starring Taron Egerton as the bow-wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.940
rogue, it bombed at the box office. But&nbsp;
in the aftermath of the movie's failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.820
Egerton was quick to throw blame&nbsp;
and defend his part in the film.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.460
In an interview with Variety, Egerton claimed,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.960
"It was absolutely not the movie that I signed up&nbsp;
to make. It was pitched to me in a different way."

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.220
What that way could have been we're not&nbsp;
sure, but perhaps they left the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.240
gritty reboot part out when selling him&nbsp;
on the film. Whatever the case may be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.727
the star believed the film's problems&nbsp;
boiled down to excessive studio meddling.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.430
"What are you doing?"
"My duty, Loxley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.600
We have new orders from above. Like it or&nbsp;
not, soldier, we're all powerless here."

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.300
Egerton explained what he&nbsp;
thought happened, saying,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.840
"I think it was made by committee&nbsp;
and I think it lost its vision. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.140
wasn't very happy on set. I didn't&nbsp;
have a very happy time making it."

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:26.100
Arguably one of the most infamous comic book&nbsp;
flops, 2004's Catwoman started out in the 1990s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.360
as a spin-off featuring Michelle Pfeiffer's&nbsp;
character from Batman Returns. But after years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.400
in development hell, actress Halle Berry took&nbsp;
over the role. A critical and box office bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.648
the film took home a number of "worst"&nbsp;
awards at the Razzies the following year.

00:01:39.648 --> 00:01:44.880
"White Russian. No ice. Hold&nbsp;
the vodka, hold the Kahlua."

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.440
Regardless, star Halle Berry has never been&nbsp;
ashamed of this one, even showing up in person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.460
to collect her "Worst Actress" Razzie.&nbsp;
In fact, she's always defended the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.900
and had plenty to say about it in response to&nbsp;
its harshest critics, telling the Daily Mail,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.800
"It was definitely different from any&nbsp;
other [movie] I had seen. I thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.560
we needed a better villain, but&nbsp;
I was outnumbered in that area."

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.980
Like Egerton on Robin Hood,&nbsp;
Berry also talked about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.160
struggles of assessing a project&nbsp;
even after it's produced, saying,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.000
"You just never know how people are going&nbsp;
to respond. [For example] I thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.780
Monster's Ball was going to end my career.&nbsp;
And look what happened. You just never know."

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.340
You never know is right: Berry took home an&nbsp;
Academy Award for her work in Monster's Ball.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:30.360
If Catwoman was the biggest comic book movie bust&nbsp;
of the 2000s, Morbius is its 2020s successor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.880
Centered on the titular vampire anti-hero played&nbsp;
by Jared Leto, Morbius was savaged by critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.600
becoming the butt of jokes that helped&nbsp;
fuel long-running Internet memes.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.380
But despite the negative reaction&nbsp;
and lack of box office bucks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.130
Matt Smith, who played the movie's&nbsp;
villain, didn't let it get him down.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:51.450
"I will not go back. You cannot make me&nbsp;
go back. I won't let you make me go back."

00:02:53.904 --> 00:02:56.304
The former Doctor Who star told Rolling Stone,

00:02:56.304 --> 00:03:01.020
"It was thrown under the bus. But you just have&nbsp;
to roll with it. What else are you gonna do?"

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.720
Unwilling to be dragged&nbsp;
down by the film's failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.100
Smith insisted that everything be put in&nbsp;
the proper perspective. He elaborated,

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.240
"It's a film, at the end of the day, we're&nbsp;
not saving lives. For whatever reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.508
it didn't quite work out and – It is what it is."

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.300
Can we really call Black Adam a bomb? It certainly&nbsp;
wasn't the hit studio execs were expecting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.640
not even pulling in $400 million on a reported&nbsp;
$200 million dollar budget. Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.620
Wakanda Forever amassed more domestically&nbsp;
in just three days than Black Adam did in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.060
its entire run — and the DC film fell&nbsp;
to fourth place in less than 30 days.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.440
But Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson got defensive&nbsp;
at the mere suggestion that his movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.300
compared unfavorably to Wakanda Forever, its&nbsp;
major rival at the box office. He tweeted —

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.620
"There's no competition with the established&nbsp;
global brand of Black Panther compared to Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.600
Adam &amp; JSA who a year ago no one even heard&nbsp;
of. [...] We're new babies and have to grow."

00:03:54.744 --> 00:03:59.964
"There is no 'we' here. There are&nbsp;
only heroes, and there are villains."

00:03:59.964 --> 00:04:05.100
Meanwhile, when Variety outright called the film&nbsp;
a bust, Johnson pushed back on Twitter, saying,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.300
"Our film will PROFIT [...] we are&nbsp;
building our new franchise step by step."

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.340
Not long after, Johnson reportedly leaked&nbsp;
a misleading financial statement to refute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:19.573
claims of the film losing money — an unusual&nbsp;
tactic for a major star to say the least.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:26.160
Cats was a celebrated Tony Award-winning Broadway&nbsp;
musical. That it wasn't adapted to film until 2019&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.580
is actually somewhat surprising. It would have&nbsp;
made a delightful Disney animated movie. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:38.160
audiences got a live-action retelling starring the&nbsp;
likes of Judi Dench, Ian McKellan, Taylor Swift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.860
and Idris Elba, all digitally dolled-up as&nbsp;
bizarre anthropomorphic human-cat hybrids.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.300
Critics and audiences were baffled and&nbsp;
horrified in equal measure, and the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.160
was a major disaster all the way around. But&nbsp;
the cast never wavered — Jennifer Hudson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.760
who played Grizabella in the film,&nbsp;
was particularly defensive in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.740
face of harsh critical response. In a 2021&nbsp;
interview with Total Film, Hudson said,

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.820
"You know what? I think it was a bit&nbsp;
overwhelming. It's unfortunate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.540
it was misunderstood. I think later down&nbsp;
the line, people will see it differently."

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:15.060
But whether the film improves with age or remains&nbsp;
a misfire, Hudson has no regrets, claiming,

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.300
"It is something I am still very&nbsp;
proud of and grateful to have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.360
a part of. Yeah, I got to be&nbsp;
Grizabella the Glamour Cat!"

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.540
In the late 2000s, sci-fi and fantasy franchises&nbsp;
were delivering blockbuster box office returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.880
and Disney wanted to kickstart one of their own.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:33.727
"Carter. John Carter."

00:05:33.727 --> 00:05:39.060
In 2012, the studio released the ill-fated&nbsp;
flop John Carter. It was slammed by critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.840
for its confusing story, incoherent&nbsp;
plot, and nonsensical characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.100
becoming one of the biggest box&nbsp;
office disasters of the 2000s.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:52.080
If anyone had reason to be angry, it would be&nbsp;
Taylor Kitsch, who played the titular Carter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.860
But despite all the derision the movie&nbsp;
received and the money the film lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.800
Kitsch maintained that it&nbsp;
wasn't a flop at all, saying,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.300
"I don't see it as a failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.020
that's the thing. I'm incredibly proud&nbsp;
of it and I would do it all over again."

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.800
Pointing out that the film still&nbsp;
sold plenty of tickets, Kitsch added,

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.140
"If someone told me that my first lead film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.860
will make well over 300 million&nbsp;
dollars, that's a good thing."

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.460
But the actor was most defensive&nbsp;
about his own role in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:21.180
insisting any shortcomings had&nbsp;
nothing to do with him. He alleged,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.080
"I know personally I literally did&nbsp;
everything possible I could have in John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:30.240
Carter. [...] That's why I prep so hard, and why&nbsp;
I push myself so hard, so I can have no regrets."

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:36.780
Another notorious money pit, Gigli, was a romantic&nbsp;
crime thriller from 2003 starring Ben Affleck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:41.580
and Jennifer Lopez, about a pair of crooks who&nbsp;
screw up a kidnapping plot. Affleck and Lopez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:46.200
began dating during production, which prompted the&nbsp;
studio to rewrite the script. The movie ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:51.204
being a total disaster, earning back a fraction&nbsp;
of its budget and getting roasted by critics.

00:06:51.204 --> 00:06:52.822
"In every relationship, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.822 --> 00:06:57.353
a bull and a cow. I'm the bull.&nbsp;
You're the cow. You got that?"

00:06:57.353 --> 00:07:01.200
Affleck blamed the studio for playing up the&nbsp;
publicity around their real-life romance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.640
telling Entertainment Weekly,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:06.960
"Because I had begun having this relationship&nbsp;
with Jennifer Lopez, which was selling a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.460
of magazines and appeared to generate a lot of&nbsp;
enthusiasm, they just predictably latched onto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.540
'They want a romantic comedy. They want&nbsp;
the two of them together. More of that!'"

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.520
But Lopez went further, openly admitting&nbsp;
that the film sucked. In an appearance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.060
Late Night with Seth Meyers, Lopez&nbsp;
got a bit defensive when pressed.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.440
"There's worse movies than 'Gigli' out there, OK?"

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.820
But Lopez, like Affleck, admitted that&nbsp;
their relationship, which was getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:36.660
all the attention during filming, was&nbsp;
at least part of the film's problem.

00:07:36.801 --> 00:07:41.340
"I know! I know! We got a lot of crap at that time. It was because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.054
we were together. It was a whole&nbsp;
other thing going on as well."

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.300
Adapted from the iconic TV series, The&nbsp;
Lone Ranger was thought to be the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.800
big franchise for Disney and Johnny Depp,&nbsp;
who'd had blockbuster success with Pirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.320
of the Caribbean. Lone Ranger was anything but&nbsp;
a blockbuster, however, losing an estimated $200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.720
million. But that didn't seem to bother the film's&nbsp;
stars, Johnny Depp and Armie Hammer, who brushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.220
off any suggestion that the movie had failed.&nbsp;
In an interview with Yahoo! Movies, Depp said,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:11.640
"I think the reviews were written&nbsp;
seven-to-eight months before we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:15.720
released the film. I think the reviews were&nbsp;
written when they heard Gore [Verbinski] and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.860
Jerry [Bruckheimer] and me were going to do&nbsp;
The Lone Ranger. They had expectations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.204
it must be a blockbuster. I didn't have&nbsp;
any expectations of that. I never do."

00:08:24.204 --> 00:08:28.944
"I'm a spirit warrior. I can't miss."

00:08:28.944 --> 00:08:33.060
Hammer, meanwhile, was more feisty in&nbsp;
his defense of the film, asserting that,

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:37.320
"If you go back and read the negative reviews,&nbsp;
most of them aren't about the content of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.520
movie, but more what's behind it. It's got&nbsp;
to the point with American critics where if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.780
you're not as smart as Plato, you're stupid.&nbsp;
That seems like a sad way to live your life."

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:50.400
Hammer believes the press piled on&nbsp;
in a critical feeding frenzy, adding,

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.820
"I think it was the popular thing&nbsp;
when the movie hit rocky terrain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.280
they jumped on the bandwagon to try and bash it."

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.800
Although the thriller Serenity boasted&nbsp;
a stellar cast, it launched in 2019 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.520
little fanfare. In addition to stars&nbsp;
Matthew McConaughey and Anne Hathaway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.360
it also featured Diane Lane,&nbsp;
Jason Clarke, Djimon Hounsou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.220
and Jeremy Strong. The film tells the story of&nbsp;
a divorced boat captain who reluctantly comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.607
the aid of his ex-wife when she wants to have&nbsp;
her abusive husband taken out of the picture.

00:09:18.607 --> 00:09:23.707
"I want you to take him out on your boat&nbsp;
and drop him in the ocean for the sharks."

00:09:23.707 --> 00:09:25.920
Unfortunately, the movie failed to connect with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.280
critics or audiences and didn't&nbsp;
even make back its modest budget.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:32.460
But Hathaway wasn't deterred by the&nbsp;
film's poor critical or commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.120
reception and took to social media&nbsp;
to defend her work. She posted:

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.740
"We keep it real around here. Some critics get&nbsp;
Serenity and, like me, find it interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.825
moving, ambitious and different, and for&nbsp;
some it just doesn't work. That's cool."

00:09:45.825 --> 00:09:48.194
"Sometimes we do bad things for good reasons."

00:09:48.194 --> 00:09:51.420
Hathaway appeared to accept that&nbsp;
not everyone has to like a project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:55.320
but her defense was more than just&nbsp;
a fair-minded retort. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:58.380
the actress seemed to take personal&nbsp;
offense to the bad reviews, writing,

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:03.180
"There is no failure, only learned events, not&nbsp;
everyone has to like everything, and the critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:08.100
response doesn't change my feelings about the&nbsp;
movie. However, other people's time and money are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:13.573
not to be taken for granted, so I just want to be&nbsp;
clear: I endorse the movie, many critics don’t."

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.780
Produced by Judd Apatow, Bros is a romantic&nbsp;
comedy written by and starring comedian Billy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:23.700
Eichner. It focuses on two men who are decidedly&nbsp;
not looking for commitment, but nevertheless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:29.520
fall in love. Despite praise for its LGBTQ&nbsp;
cast including Luke Macfarlane, Ts Madison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.680
and Eichner himself, the film bombed at the&nbsp;
box office. But unlike most flops on this list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:40.140
Bros received critical acclaim for its clever&nbsp;
comedy, earnest story, and heart. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.920
Eichner was more than just disappointed&nbsp;
by the film's take at the ticket counter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.640
as he lashed out at both critics and at&nbsp;
audiences while defending its failure.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.120
In some since-deleted Twitter&nbsp;
comments, Eichner put the blame on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.960
homophobic audiences who refused to show&nbsp;
up for the well-reviewed film, tweeting:

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:57.960
"Straight people, especially in&nbsp;
certain parts of the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:03.367
just didn't show up for Bros. Everyone who ISN'T&nbsp;
a homophobic weirdo should go see Bros tonight."

00:11:03.367 --> 00:11:07.920
"Lesbian history month was in&nbsp;
March. Nobody said a goddamn thing."

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.780
But despite his emotional outburst, Eichner&nbsp;
did make a few valid points, writing,

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.020
"Rolling Stone already has BROS&nbsp;
on the list of the best comedies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:20.100
of the 21st century. What's also true is&nbsp;
that at one point a theater chain called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.340
Universal said they were pulling the&nbsp;
trailer because of the gay content."

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.700
Although the studio convinced the&nbsp;
theater to continue running the trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:31.020
evidence suggests that we still&nbsp;
have a long way to go before LGBTQ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:35.100
content is completely accepted and&nbsp;
celebrated by the American moviegoer.

